00:22:54.000 --> 00:23:13.000
Whitney Hodges: So on the Meta ad side, it's a bit different, right? Because Google search platform, right? People are going there, they're searching with high intent, they're looking for, you know, a videographer, whereas on Meta, they're going on there for a variety of reasons. It could just be to scroll and be entertained, it could be to connect with.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:32.000
Whitney Hodges: friends and family. So, on that platform, it really is more about disruptor marketing, stopping the scroll, capturing people's, um, attention. And like you saw when you ran meta ads before, um, you can end up paying a lot of money just for visibility, right?

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:50.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, it's pay-per-impression on the meta ad side versus Google, where it's pay-per-click. So, on the meta ad side, it's so super important that the copy is really dialed in, that the ad creative is very scroll-stopping, and to run that full funnel of ads that I was mentioning that has.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:24:06.000
Whitney Hodges: ads that run to cold traffic, ads that run for lead generation, and ads that run as retargeting, there's a different… there's different types of content, basically, that you want to be running at each stage. Different objectives, different way the audiences are set up.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:25.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, that, you know, all of that, that kind of goes into it. So, inside the accelerator, we do have some education on what these different types of campaigns are, and why, as well as what makes good content for each stage of the funnel, with lots of examples and lookbooks for you, so that.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:42.000
Whitney Hodges: you can kind of, you know, look at your work, your gallery, your reels, or things like that, and pull together ad content for each phase of the funnel. So that is a part of it, you know, that you would be responsible for, right, putting together the content of the ads themselves.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:59.000
Whitney Hodges: But then you can submit that to us for feedback, right? We can let you know if we would suggest any tweaks or changes or anything like that. And then when you're ready for the ads to actually go live, there's a really simple submission process where you fill out a form, you know, include any kind of relevant links.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Whitney Hodges: And then behind the scenes, we actually go into your ad account, we set everything up and get the ads launched for you. So that's kind of the done for you component of the meta ad side. Us actually doing all the work inside your account, you know, making sure tracking is in place, that audiences are right.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:23.000
Whitney Hodges: That all the settings are what we would suggest, um, but you're more responsible for the content itself.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, we like doing it that way because we find that once you have campaigns that are in there that are set up the right way, it's so much easier on your end to go in and, like, duplicate a campaign or duplicate an ad, right, and make tweaks or changes for yourself moving forward than it is to have to, like, start from scratch.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:49.000
